2026-06-30 15:20:33,625 - openai._base_client - DEBUG - Request options: {'method': 'post', 'url': '/chat/completions', 'timeout': 300, 'files': None, 'idempotency_key': 'stainless-python-retry-d8656239-106a-4bff-86a8-af024b1415ea', 'security': {'bearer_auth': True}, 'content': None, 'json_data': {'messages': [{'role': 'system', 'content': 'You are an expert proteomics assistant. Return valid JSON only.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'You are a proteomics expert analyzing crosslinking mass spectrometry (XL-MS) datasets.\n\nYour task is to cluster raw spectral files into biologically meaningful groups.\n\nA cluster should represent a unique combination of:\n1. Biological / experimental condition\n   - Examples: treatment, control, carbon source, growth medium, cell state, tissue, disease state, time point, perturbation, purified complex, recombinant standard set.\n2. Source organism of the proteome being analyzed\n   - Return the full scientific name.\n   - Use the organism of the biological sample or proteome of interest, not necessarily the expression host.\n   - Example: if human proteins are expressed in E. coli and analyzed as human recombinant standards, the source organism is Homo sapiens.\n   - If the dataset intentionally contains multiple source organisms, create separate clusters by organism when possible.\n3. Crosslinking reagent\n   - Examples: BS3, DSS, DSSO, DSBU, formaldehyde.\n   - If multiple crosslinkers are used, create separate clusters by reagent when possible.\n\nDo NOT split clusters by:\n- fraction number\n- LC fraction\n- technical replicate\n- biological replicate ID\n- injection number\n- file numbering\n- enrichment fraction\n- instrument run order\n- search batch\n- calibration/QC files\n\nReplicates, fractions, and repeated injections from the same biological condition should be grouped together.\n\n---\n\nINPUTS\n\nRaw filenames:\nZou_Rappsilber_JP_CPC_Sgo1_EDC_R1.raw\nZou_Rappsilber_JP_CPC_Sgo1_EDC_R2.raw\nZou_Rappsilber_JP_CPC_Sgo1_EDC_R3.raw\n\nPublication text and PRIDE metadata:\n[TITLE]\nMolecular Basis for CPC-Sgo1 Interaction: Implications for Centromere Localisation and Function of the CPCMolecular Basis for CPC-Sgo1 Interaction: Implications for Centromere Localisation and Function of the CPC\n\n[ABSTRACT]\nSgo1 makes multipartite interactions with CPC subunits Previous studies have suggested that the Sgo1-CPC (Chromosomal Passenger Complex) interaction is mediated via the N-terminal coiled-coil of Sgo1 and Borealin. However, our structural data revealed that the very N-terminus of Sgo1 can interact with the BIR domain of Survivin. Together, these studies suggest that multi-partite interactions between Sgo1 and different CPC subunits could facilitate CPC-Sgo1 complex formation. To gain further structural insights, we performed chemical cross-linking of the CPCISB10-280-Sgo11-415 complex using a zero-length cross-linker, 1-ethyl-3-(3-dimethylaminopropyl) carbodiimide (EDC), followed by mass spectrometry analysis. Cross-linking-mass spectrometry (CLMS) data showed that: (1) consistent with our previous observations,  the N-terminal region of Sgo1 (amino acids 1-34) makes extensive contacts with Survivin BIR domain (amino acids 18-89); (2) the N-terminal coiled-coil of Sgo1 (amino acids 10-120)  interacts with the CPC triple helical bundle; (3) consistent with previous findings, the N-terminal coiled-coil also contacts the Borealin dimerisation domain; and (4) the Sgo1 region beyond the N-terminal coil-coil region, which is predicted to be unstructured, contacts both Survivin and Borealin with most contacts confined to the Sgo1 central region spanning amino acids (aa) 180-300. Thus, our cross-linking results suggests that Sgo1 interacts with CPC, mainly via two regions, the N-terminal coiled-coil domain and the unstructured central region.\n\n[METHODS]\nChemical cross-linking and MS analysis Cross-linking experiments of Sgo11-415 and CPCISB10-280 were performed using  EDC (Thermo Fisher Scientific) in the presence of N-hydroxysulfosuccinimide (NHS, Thermo Fisher Scientific). 25 \uf06dg of gel filtrated protein complex was cross-linked with 120 \uf06dg EDC and 44 \uf06dg of NHS in 25 mM HEPES pH 6.8, 150 mM NaCl for 1h 30min at room temperature. The cross-linking was stopped by the addition of 100 mM Tris-HCl and cross-linking products were briefly resolved using a 4-12 % Bis-Tris NuPAGE (Thermo Fisher Scientific). Bands were visualised by short Instant Blue staining (Abcam), excised, reduced with 10 mM DTT for 30 min at room temperature, alkylated with 5 mM iodoacetamide for 20 min at room temperature and digested overnight at 37 °C using 13 ng/\uf06dl trypsin (Promega). Digested peptides were loaded onto C18-Stage-tips. LC-MS/MS analysis was performed using an Orbitrap Fusion™ Lumos™ Tribrid™ Mass Spectrometer (Thermo Scientific) applying a “high-high” acquisition strategy. Peptide mixtures were injected for each mass spectrometric acquisition. Peptides were separated on a 75 µm x 50 cm PepMap EASY-Spray column (Thermo Scientific) fitted into an EASY-Spray source (Thermo Scientific), operated at 50 ºC column temperature. Mobile phase A consisted of water and 0.1% v/v formic acid. Mobile phase B consisted of 80% v/v acetonitrile and 0.1% v/v formic acid. Peptides were loaded at a flow-rate of 0.3 μl/min and eluted at 0.2 μl/min using a linear gradient going from 2% mobile phase B to 40% mobile phase B over 139 (or 109) minutes, followed by a linear increase from 40% to 95% mobile phase B in 11 minutes. The eluted peptides were directly introduced into the mass spectrometer. MS data were acquired in the data-dependent mode with the top-speed option. For each three-second acquisition cycle, the mass spectrum was recorded in the Orbitrap with a resolution of 120,000. The ions with a precursor charge state between 3+ and 8+ were isolated and fragmented using HCD or EThcD. The fragmentation spectra were recorded in the Orbitrap. Dynamic exclusion was enabled with single repeat count and 60-second exclusion duration.\n\n\nPreviously extracted organism(s) and NCBI taxid(s):\nHomo sapiens (human)\n\n---\n\nTASKS\n\n1. Identify the distinct biological/experimental conditions described by the publication, PRIDE metadata, and filenames.\n\n2. Assign every raw file to exactly one cluster.\n\n3. Define clusters using the smallest number of biologically meaningful groups. Do not over-cluster based only on replicate, fraction, or run-number differences.\n\n4. For each cluster, report:\n   - cluster_id: "cluster_1", "cluster_2", etc.\n   - cluster_label: short machine-readable label using lowercase words separated by underscores\n   - description: concise human-readable summary of the condition\n   - organism: full scientific name of the proteome/sample organism\n   - taxid: NCBI Taxonomy CURIE, e.g. "NCBITaxon:9606"; use null if unknown\n   - crosslinker: crosslinking reagent; use null if unknown\n   - biological_condition: short phrase describing the treatment/control/state\n   - files: list of raw filenames assigned to this cluster\n   - confidence: "high", "medium", or "low"\n   - evidence_quote: exact sentence or short phrase from the publication, PRIDE metadata, or filename pattern that supports this cluster assignment\n   - reasoning: brief explanation of why these files belong together\n\n5. Assign files that cannot be confidently interpreted to cluster_0 with:\n   - cluster_label: "unassigned"\n   - confidence: "low"\n   - evidence_quote: null\n   - reasoning: explain why assignment was not possible\n\n6. QC, calibrant, blank, wash, or test files should be assigned to cluster_0 unless the publication clearly states they are part of the biological experiment.\n\n7. If all biological files appear to come from one condition, create only cluster_1 plus cluster_0 if there are unassigned/QC files.\n\n8. Confidence guidelines:\n   - "high": filenames and publication/metadata agree clearly\n   - "medium": assignment is likely but partially inferred from filenames or incomplete metadata\n   - "low": weak evidence, ambiguous naming, missing metadata, or conflicting information\n\n9. If publication text and filenames conflict, prioritize the publication/PRIDE metadata, but mention the conflict in reasoning.\n\n10. Return ONLY valid JSON. Do not include markdown, comments, or explanatory text outside the JSON.\n\n---\n\nOUTPUT JSON STRUCTURE\n\n{\n  "clusters": [\n    {\n      "cluster_id": "cluster_1",\n      "cluster_label": "yeast_glucose_bs3",\n      "description": "Saccharomyces cerevisiae samples grown in glucose-rich medium and crosslinked with BS3.",\n      "organism": "Saccharomyces cerevisiae",\n      "taxid": "NCBITaxon:4932",\n      "crosslinker": "BS3",\n      "biological_condition": "glucose-rich medium",\n      "files": [\n        "YeastGlu_R1.raw",\n        "YeastGlu_R2.raw",\n        "YeastGlu_fraction_01.raw"\n      ],\n      "confidence": "high",\n      "evidence_quote": "Yeast cells were cultured in glucose- or glycerol-containing medium and crosslinked with BS3.",\n      "reasoning": "The filenames contain Glu and the publication states that glucose-grown yeast samples were crosslinked with BS3."\n    },\n    {\n      "cluster_id": "cluster_0",\n      "cluster_label": "unassigned",\n      "description": "Files that could not be confidently assigned to a biological XL-MS condition.",\n      "organism": null,\n      "taxid": null,\n      "crosslinker": null,\n      "biological_condition": null,\n      "files": [\n        "calibrant.raw",\n        "QC.raw"\n      ],\n      "confidence": "low",\n      "evidence_quote": null,\n      "reasoning": "These files appear to be QC or calibration runs rather than biological samples."\n    }\n  ],\n  "summary": {\n    "n_input_files": 5,\n    "n_assigned_files": 3,\n    "n_unassigned_files": 2,\n    "n_biological_clusters": 1,\n    "overall_confidence": "high"\n  }\n}'}], 'model': 'gpt-5.4-nano-2026-03-17', 'store': True, 'temperature': 0.3}}
