2026-06-30 15:20:46,010 - openai._base_client - DEBUG - Request options: {'method': 'post', 'url': '/chat/completions', 'timeout': 300, 'files': None, 'idempotency_key': 'stainless-python-retry-0f7794ab-599c-4dc8-abba-8d34e164b033', 'security': {'bearer_auth': True}, 'content': None, 'json_data': {'messages': [{'role': 'system', 'content': 'You are an expert SDRF-Proteomics annotator for crosslinking mass spectrometry (XL-MS) datasets.\n\nExtract the following SDRF metadata terms related to the mass spectrometry acquisition.\n\nTERMS TO EXTRACT:\n─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\nterm: instrument\nsdrf_column: comment[instrument]\nontology: MS:1000031 (PSI-MS)\ndescription: Mass spectrometer instrument make and model.\ninstructions: Use PSI-MS ontology. If multiple instruments, return a list.\n  Common instruments (name → accession):\n    Orbitrap Astral → MS:1003356\n    Orbitrap Exploris 480 → MS:1003028\n    Orbitrap Eclipse → MS:1003349\n    Orbitrap Fusion Lumos → MS:1002732\n    Orbitrap Fusion → MS:1002416\n    Q Exactive HF-X → MS:1002877\n    Q Exactive HF → MS:1002523\n    Q Exactive Plus → MS:1002634\n    Q Exactive → MS:1001911\n    LTQ Orbitrap Velos → MS:1001742\n    LTQ Orbitrap XL → MS:1000556\n    timsTOF → MS:1003005\n    timsTOF Pro → MS:1003230\n    TripleTOF 6600 → MS:1002583\n  Format: NT=<instrument name>;AC=<MS accession>\n\nterm: proteomics data acquisition method\nsdrf_column: comment[proteomics data acquisition method]\nontology: PRIDE:0000659\ndescription: Mass spectrometry data acquisition method.\ninstructions: For XL-MS this is nearly always DDA. Use:\n  NT=data-dependent acquisition;AC=PRIDE:0000041 — for DDA/shotgun\n  NT=data-independent acquisition;AC=PRIDE:0000449 — for DIA (rare in XL-MS)\n  NT=targeted acquisition;AC=PRIDE:0000450 — for SRM/PRM\n  Default to DDA if not stated.\n\nterm: dissociation method\nsdrf_column: comment[dissociation method]\nontology: MS:1000044 (PSI-MS children)\ndescription: Fragmentation method used for MS/MS.\ninstructions: Use PSI-MS terms. If multiple methods, return a list.\n  Common methods:\n    HCD → NT=HCD;AC=MS:1000422\n    CID → NT=CID;AC=MS:1002472\n    ETD → NT=ETD;AC=MS:1000598\n    EThcD → NT=EThcD;AC=MS:1002678\n    UVPD → NT=UVPD;AC=MS:1003247\n    stepped HCD → NT=HCD;AC=MS:1000422 (stepped HCD is still HCD)\n\nterm: collision energy\nsdrf_column: comment[collision energy]\nontology: MS:1000045\ndescription: Collision energy used for fragmentation.\npattern: {value} {unit} where unit is NCE, %, or eV.\n  Stepped HCD: {value} {unit};{value} {unit};{value} {unit}\n  Examples: "30 NCE", "30% NCE", "27 eV", "25 NCE;27 NCE;30 NCE"\ninstructions: Return null if not stated.\n\nterm: dia method\nsdrf_column: comment[dia method]\nontology: PRIDE:0000450\ndescription: Specific DIA method variant (only relevant for DIA datasets).\ninstructions: Return null for DDA XL-MS datasets (which is nearly all XL-MS).\n  If DIA is used, specify the variant (e.g. "SWATH", "MSe", "diaPASEF").\n\nterm: fragment mass tolerance\nsdrf_column: comment[fragment mass tolerance]\nontology: PRIDE:0000576\ndescription: Fragment ion mass tolerance for the database search.\npattern: number + ppm or Da (e.g. "20 ppm", "0.02 Da")\ninstructions: Return null if not stated. Typical XL-MS values: 10–20 ppm for\n  Orbitrap instruments, 0.02–0.05 Da for ion trap.\n\nterm: precursor mass tolerance\nsdrf_column: comment[precursor mass tolerance]\nontology: PRIDE:0000575\ndescription: Precursor ion mass tolerance for the database search.\npattern: number + ppm or Da (e.g. "10 ppm", "5 ppm")\ninstructions: Return null if not stated. Typical: 5–10 ppm.\n\nterm: technical replicate\nsdrf_column: comment[technical replicate]\nontology: MS:1001808\ndescription: Identifier for the technical replicate (integer).\ninstructions: Describe the technical replication strategy found in the text\n  (e.g. "2 technical replicates per sample", "no technical replicates stated").\n  Actual integers are assigned per-file automatically.\n\nterm: data file\nsdrf_column: comment[data file]\nontology: MS:1003083\ndescription: Name of the raw data file (.raw, .wiff, .d).\ninstructions: This is always auto-derived from the PRIDE file list.\n  Describe the file naming convention you observe (e.g. "sample_condition_rep.raw").\n─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n\nReturn a single JSON object where each key is the exact sdrf_column string shown above.\nFor each key, return a dict with:\n  "value": the extracted value (string, list, or null)\n  "accession": ontology accession if resolvable, else null\n  "confidence": "high", "medium", or "low"\n  "evidence_quote": short verbatim quote from the text, or null\n  "evidence_location": location in the paper (e.g. "Methods"), or null'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': '[TITLE]\nMolecular Basis for CPC-Sgo1 Interaction: Implications for Centromere Localisation and Function of the CPCMolecular Basis for CPC-Sgo1 Interaction: Implications for Centromere Localisation and Function of the CPC\n\n[ABSTRACT]\nSgo1 makes multipartite interactions with CPC subunits Previous studies have suggested that the Sgo1-CPC (Chromosomal Passenger Complex) interaction is mediated via the N-terminal coiled-coil of Sgo1 and Borealin. However, our structural data revealed that the very N-terminus of Sgo1 can interact with the BIR domain of Survivin. Together, these studies suggest that multi-partite interactions between Sgo1 and different CPC subunits could facilitate CPC-Sgo1 complex formation. To gain further structural insights, we performed chemical cross-linking of the CPCISB10-280-Sgo11-415 complex using a zero-length cross-linker, 1-ethyl-3-(3-dimethylaminopropyl) carbodiimide (EDC), followed by mass spectrometry analysis. Cross-linking-mass spectrometry (CLMS) data showed that: (1) consistent with our previous observations,  the N-terminal region of Sgo1 (amino acids 1-34) makes extensive contacts with Survivin BIR domain (amino acids 18-89); (2) the N-terminal coiled-coil of Sgo1 (amino acids 10-120)  interacts with the CPC triple helical bundle; (3) consistent with previous findings, the N-terminal coiled-coil also contacts the Borealin dimerisation domain; and (4) the Sgo1 region beyond the N-terminal coil-coil region, which is predicted to be unstructured, contacts both Survivin and Borealin with most contacts confined to the Sgo1 central region spanning amino acids (aa) 180-300. Thus, our cross-linking results suggests that Sgo1 interacts with CPC, mainly via two regions, the N-terminal coiled-coil domain and the unstructured central region.\n\n[METHODS]\nChemical cross-linking and MS analysis Cross-linking experiments of Sgo11-415 and CPCISB10-280 were performed using  EDC (Thermo Fisher Scientific) in the presence of N-hydroxysulfosuccinimide (NHS, Thermo Fisher Scientific). 25 \uf06dg of gel filtrated protein complex was cross-linked with 120 \uf06dg EDC and 44 \uf06dg of NHS in 25 mM HEPES pH 6.8, 150 mM NaCl for 1h 30min at room temperature. The cross-linking was stopped by the addition of 100 mM Tris-HCl and cross-linking products were briefly resolved using a 4-12 % Bis-Tris NuPAGE (Thermo Fisher Scientific). Bands were visualised by short Instant Blue staining (Abcam), excised, reduced with 10 mM DTT for 30 min at room temperature, alkylated with 5 mM iodoacetamide for 20 min at room temperature and digested overnight at 37 °C using 13 ng/\uf06dl trypsin (Promega). Digested peptides were loaded onto C18-Stage-tips. LC-MS/MS analysis was performed using an Orbitrap Fusion™ Lumos™ Tribrid™ Mass Spectrometer (Thermo Scientific) applying a “high-high” acquisition strategy. Peptide mixtures were injected for each mass spectrometric acquisition. Peptides were separated on a 75 µm x 50 cm PepMap EASY-Spray column (Thermo Scientific) fitted into an EASY-Spray source (Thermo Scientific), operated at 50 ºC column temperature. Mobile phase A consisted of water and 0.1% v/v formic acid. Mobile phase B consisted of 80% v/v acetonitrile and 0.1% v/v formic acid. Peptides were loaded at a flow-rate of 0.3 μl/min and eluted at 0.2 μl/min using a linear gradient going from 2% mobile phase B to 40% mobile phase B over 139 (or 109) minutes, followed by a linear increase from 40% to 95% mobile phase B in 11 minutes. The eluted peptides were directly introduced into the mass spectrometer. MS data were acquired in the data-dependent mode with the top-speed option. For each three-second acquisition cycle, the mass spectrum was recorded in the Orbitrap with a resolution of 120,000. The ions with a precursor charge state between 3+ and 8+ were isolated and fragmented using HCD or EThcD. The fragmentation spectra were recorded in the Orbitrap. Dynamic exclusion was enabled with single repeat count and 60-second exclusion duration.\n'}], 'model': 'gpt-5.4-nano-2026-03-17', 'store': True, 'temperature': 0.3}}
